INT. PLANETARIUM - DOME CHAMBER - NIGHT

Concrete ring under a hemispherical ceiling. Tilted seats. A star projector crouches in the center like a black insect.

A FIGURE in coveralls, faceless behind a balaclava, clips a harness to the projector's rail. Quick, practiced. A radio crackles at his hip.

A second FIGURE (a woman, short, headlamp off) climbs the access ladder with a canvas bag.

FIGURE ONE
Eleven minutes.

FIGURE TWO
Ten.

She unzips the bag. Inside, a glass cylinder wrapped in foam: the meteorite on loan from Geneva.

Footsteps in the corridor outside. A key ring jingles. Both freeze. Her hand lands on his sleeve.

Neither moves. His breathing slows. Her thumb presses his wrist, once, twice.

The footsteps recede. They exhale together.

FIGURE ONE
(pulling off the balaclava)
Name's Kiran. Since we're doing this.

FIGURE TWO
(pulling off hers)
Marisol.

KIRAN
Don't tell me the rest.

She wraps the cylinder. He doesn't look at it. He looks at the dome, where a single painted star glows, forgotten by the maintenance crew.
